WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.152 --> 00:00:02.985
(slow rock music)

00:00:05.736 --> 00:00:08.403
- [Brian] I have to own my size.

00:00:11.967 --> 00:00:12.896
I can't go and be like,

00:00:12.896 --> 00:00:14.299
"Well, I wanna trade my body in.

00:00:14.299 --> 00:00:15.213
I wanna be smaller."

00:00:15.213 --> 00:00:16.880
It's not gonna work.

00:00:18.233 --> 00:00:19.733
Right now I'm 6'8.

00:00:20.982 --> 00:00:22.732
And about 400 pounds.

00:00:29.558 --> 00:00:30.884
My name is Brian Shaw.

00:00:30.884 --> 00:00:32.622
And I'm the World's Strongest Man.

00:00:32.622 --> 00:00:35.205
(upbeat music)

00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:39.770
The heaviest I would
compete is about 420 pounds.

00:00:39.770 --> 00:00:40.603
- [Interviewer] What do you have to do to

00:00:40.603 --> 00:00:42.920
maintain that eating wise?

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:44.805
- [Brian] To be honest,
it's a lot of eating.

00:00:44.805 --> 00:00:46.745
Gotta have the peanut butter.

00:00:46.745 --> 00:00:48.712
I'll buy at least close to ten pounds

00:00:48.712 --> 00:00:50.566
if not more of the meat.

00:00:50.566 --> 00:00:53.151
I end up pretty much buying
everything they have.

00:00:53.151 --> 00:00:56.468
You know, on a typical
day I'll probably consume

00:00:56.468 --> 00:00:59.935
at the very low end 5-6,000 calories a day

00:00:59.935 --> 00:01:02.529
and the upper end would
be about 10,000 a day.

00:01:02.529 --> 00:01:04.779
(whirring)

00:01:11.883 --> 00:01:13.000
- [Interviewer] We saw the video of you

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:14.702
going through aisle on the plane.

00:01:14.702 --> 00:01:15.535
- [Brian] Yep.

00:01:15.535 --> 00:01:16.569
- [Interviewer] Are there
any moments like that

00:01:16.569 --> 00:01:20.351
on any day-to-day that
you have to deal with?

00:01:20.351 --> 00:01:22.032
- [Brian] It does happen.

00:01:22.032 --> 00:01:23.365
Fitting different places,

00:01:23.365 --> 00:01:26.267
and it can be challenging.

00:01:26.267 --> 00:01:30.076
I find it a little bit
hard when things are tiny.

00:01:30.076 --> 00:01:33.162
Like I definitely like
my utensils to be bigger.

00:01:33.162 --> 00:01:34.919
My wife and I have different spoons,

00:01:34.919 --> 00:01:37.919
we have different forks that we use.

00:01:39.676 --> 00:01:41.070
Because my fingers are so big,

00:01:41.070 --> 00:01:43.178
typing is a little bit harder.

00:01:43.178 --> 00:01:44.990
You kind of learn to adapt to it because

00:01:44.990 --> 00:01:46.654
they're not gonna make a keyboard

00:01:46.654 --> 00:01:48.237
bigger just for me.

00:01:49.689 --> 00:01:51.544
The reason I actually
sit like a lot in these

00:01:51.544 --> 00:01:53.754
folding chairs is 'cause
a lot of office chairs

00:01:53.754 --> 00:01:57.641
have arms on 'em, you know, and I can't fit my hips,

00:01:57.641 --> 00:02:00.009
'cause you can see I'm
kinda off of this chair.

00:02:00.009 --> 00:02:01.759
So that's what works.

00:02:04.261 --> 00:02:05.763
- [Interviewer] Do
people kinda look at you

00:02:05.763 --> 00:02:08.725
and be like, "Wow, that's a huge guy."

00:02:08.725 --> 00:02:09.723
- [Brian] A lot of people do look.

00:02:09.723 --> 00:02:10.952
It's kind of funny because I don't really

00:02:10.952 --> 00:02:13.095
notice it anymore.

00:02:13.095 --> 00:02:14.169
I like being big.

00:02:14.169 --> 00:02:15.568
I've kind of embraced it.

00:02:15.568 --> 00:02:17.643
I've always been able
to do stuff with my body

00:02:17.643 --> 00:02:19.619
whether it was playing
basketball or strong man

00:02:19.619 --> 00:02:22.015
or really anything.

00:02:22.015 --> 00:02:24.598
But it's a positive thing, too.

00:02:27.168 --> 00:02:29.668
(light music)

